s your family  and it's core to your identity as a  person  um so so that's another way and then of  course  you know some amount of travel can at  least give you a feel for it  are any of the distinctions that you  study  can can value judgments be assigned to  them to some degree in terms of either  what's quote better for the individuals  within or for  the groups and societies at a whole or  or is this merely we're observing  differences and that's as far as it goes  well so my mission in the book was just  to  well to kind of start out by documenting  the differences but the thing i was  really interested in doing is explaining  the variation and cueing to my readers  many of which i sort of assumed as i as  i wrote the book would be mostly weird  so i was trying to jar them  in ways that would make them recognize  the water so to speak so recognize the  sort of psychological differences so  when i talk about analytic thinking you  know analytic thinking can be very  powerful breaking things down into parts  and assigning them properties it's  really the basis of how you know what  physics is right break everything down  into particles and give them charges or  uh spin or charge or something like that  and then explain everything from that  psychologists give peace people  personalities and try to explain that  economists do a similar thing so it's  very powerful but you can miss the  forest so like some of the subsections  in the book are like missing the forest  reminding people that if you're too much  of an analytic thinker um  you can miss how all the parts fit  together and how the relationships are  so important  so for example when you do when  psychologists do work on what leads to  effective problem solving in teams the  best teams at solving problems and  engaging in creativity are teams that  have a mixture of analytic and holistic  thinkers so you need both uh growth  groups towards the end of the book i  really emphasize the role of uh  embracing 