e they they  basically created a training course to  try to increase the self-regulation of  poor men to try to make them more  patient and more willing to defer  gratification  uh the training worked and they were  able to show that on the psychological  tests men were willing to defer  gratification more and initially this  seemed to be a big success because the  men were able to begin to develop little  nest eggs of savings and things like  that right okay so that's working but  then it turns out that because the  environment is so unstable the men would  get robbed or they'd get shaken down by  the police or something would happen and  they'd lose their complete nest egg so  they wouldn't get the pleasure that  would have come from  indulging in that  so that that's one way and you need to a  stable environment where you can  preserve that savings and you need the  chance that you're going to be alive  when you get a chance to take advantage  of it otherwise it's better to to  consume now when you're sure you're  going to get something out of it the  other thing that that varies across  societies is in a lot of cases  if i accumulate much my my relatives are  going to come and ask for it essentially  so we'll die there'll be a funeral or  there'll be a wedding um so what i do  one way to deal with a any kind of  rising surplus is just to give it away  have a party or give it to others and  the way people think about that or maybe  not consciously but the way it ends up  functioning is that you're storing your  surplus in the good will and  relationships you have with others right  when you're in need you don't have to  have a nest egg your nest egg is  scattered amongst all these  friends and relatives in close contacts  that you've cultivated over long periods  of time yeah that's super interesting  and to some degree i've studied that a  little bit in hasidic jewish communities  uh and there's a there that that  phenomena is very very notable there  interestingly enough  the book is fascinating it's called the  weirdest people in the world how the  west became psychologically peculiar and  particularly prosperous we've been  speaking with the book's author harvard  university professor joe henrich joe  really appreciate your time today  okay well it's great to be with you

